## Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for milk safety and contaminants 2 monitoring
Abstract
Everybody needs food anywhere, and milk is one of the essential parts of the diet for humans and animals. However, the increasing population has created milk safety concerns due to the excessive use of pesticides, drugs, and chemicals in agriculture. Therefore, the acute monitoring of toxicants from milk and quality will always be in demand. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) materials with tunable porosity and active functional sites are promising materials for toxicant monitoring. This review studies the insight into the electrochemical and luminescence MOF sensors for monitoring contaminants from milk samples. In addition, the MOF sensor's development, sensitivity, selectivity, and application in milk samples for detecting contaminants with sensing mechanisms and detection limits are discussed. Finally, the challenges, key findings, and future directions for MOF sensors for next-generation milk monitoring are addressed.
Keywords: MOFs; milk; luminescence; electrochemical; toxic chemicals
## 1. Introduction
Everybody needs food everywhere; safe and healthy food is the biggest concern worldwide. With the increasing population, food safety is at higher risk in developing countries and needs to prevent food pathogens [1,2]. According to the World Health Organization report, millions fall ill and die from consuming toxic contaminated food. Additionally, the poor quality is negatively impacted due to pollution from factories and the use of banned pesticides, preservatives, and other chemical species [3]. However, due to insufficient evidence of foodborne diseases, health organizations do not decide on independent action [4]. Therefore, food safety and food sample monitoring are considered critical issues of food security. Additionally, foodborne health issues through food safety incidents need to control to establish a safe food product in global food supply chains [5–7].
## 1.1 Toxicity in the food chain
Milk and milk products are usually contaminated by heavy metals, bacteria, pesticide residues, and mycotoxins [12]. These contaminants are introduced in milk through milking, cattle feed, milk processing, and pre- and post- processing period [13]. Hydrogen peroxide, formaldehyde, bronopol, azidiol, and preservatives have been used in milk industries and influence the milk protein fractions composition [14]. Mycotoxins are toxic compounds produced by certain moulds fungi and have become contaminants in dairy products. Moreover, these fungal toxins cannot be decomposed by boiling the milk. There are no effective techniques to decompose these toxic chemicals at dairy farmers and milk process levels [15,16].
## 1.2 Materials for monitoring food quality
Numerous materials for food quality monitoring have been reported these days [17,18]. The combination of various techniques such as mass spectrometry [19], micro-Raman (μRaman) [20], electrochemical, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometer (ICP-MS), spectroscopic nondestructive techniques with nanomaterials, bionanomaterials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent organic frameworks (COFs), hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks (HOFs), etc. in food quality monitoring has provided promising platforms (Figure 1) [21–29]. Different materials used for food quality monitoring are constituted with organic and inorganic functionalities [30–32]. For example, HOFs in their organic functionalities have an equal number of hydrogen bond donors and acceptors that form inherent hydrogen bond units, like dimers, trimers, and even chain structures [33,34]. COFs comprise organic building units connected with covalent bonds in their frameworks [35].
Also, Nanomaterials with various electronic properties of inorganic materials and quantum size effects have been used in the last decades for food quality monitoring [36]. Besides their porosity, MOFs have advanced feathers due to the metal centers and organic functional sites. Moreover, MOFs can be fabricated with nanomaterials, biomolecules, metal ions, carbon composites, etc., making them promising and efficient materials for monitoring food quality [37]. It is need to develop MOFs for analytical detection and pre-concentration of toxicants for food quality monitoring [38].
In this review, the food chain toxicity and milk contamination determination using MOFs are studied and discussed. The importance of electrochemical and luminescence MOFs in milk quality analysis in the global food system is described. The systematic development of MOFs and fabrication using biomolecules and metal ions, detection limits, linear ranges, and
80 recoveries are described. This study emphasizes the critical approach with the designed   
81 materials to identify the toxicants in milk.
## 2. Synthesis and properties of MOFs
Metal-organic frameworks are known for their three-dimensional crystalline structure and porosity. The functional site creation into MOFs allows for manipulating fluorescent electronic properties. Modifying ligands with electronic denser groups enhances the fluorescent properties and leads to analyte capture performances for environmental and medical applications [45]. MOFs hybrid materials with organic linkers and metal nodes that coordinate with coordination bonds have advanced structural properties [46]. The coordination of ligandmetal ions provides a multidimensional framework with porosity and active surface area. Also, the functional sites of ligands and multivalence states of metal ions can be helpful in interacting with analytes [47]. In addition, the combination of diverse composition, tailored structures, and tunable pore size have proved superior in detecting toxic chemicals in the water [48,49].
The vital knowledge to design MOFs and advanced characterization techniques enables experts to select the appropriate MOF for a desired application. Further, the additive design is beneficial for the controlled production of MOF morphologies (Figure 2) [50]. It demonstrates the transverse connection of additives with metal clusters, a pore window for crystalline structure formation  [51]. Furthermore, MOFs structural features such as porosity, defects, and ligand functionalization are boosting points for applications [52,53]. In addition, synthesis parameters such as temperature and metal precursors can reduce the  MOF synthesis time from hours to minutes  [54]. MOFs are crystalline materials that can be synthesized by layering, solvothermal, hydrothermal, etc. The choice of metal salts, ligands, and synthetic methods can result in a fine porous crystalline multidimensional MOF with advanced electronic properties. Antagonistic acid-base active sites in MOFs can be designed by ion exchange, promoting mass transfer and contacts during the organic reactions [55]. MOFs conductivity can be enhanced up to $0.028\mathrm{S/cm}$ compared to the Nafion-117 membrane with conductivity: of $0.017\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ by modifying with molecular sieving and polymeric assembly layer [56]. In addition, bi/multimetallic MOFs has been developed as doped materials with a high specific capacity of $1.77\mathrm{mAh}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ as energy materials [57].
MOFs electrochemical and luminescence activities can be affected by morphology and structural properties [58,59]. The hollow structure MOF showed better electrocatalytic activity than solid structured MOF [60]. For example, the post-synthetic modification with Au into hollow PCN-222 ( $\mathrm{\DeltaZr}$ -MOF) changed the crystalline properties and morphologies that exhibit high electrochemical activity. Moreover, the improvement resulted in the enhanced electrocatalytic surface area suitable for charge transfer coefficient. Biswas et al. designed an Au-composited MOF electrode (AuHPCN-222/GCE) that showed excellent detection for estradiol (ED) with a limit of detection (LOD) of $0.5{\mathrm{~nM}}$ with the highest sensitivity of 2.2 $\upmu\mathrm{A}/\upmu\mathrm{M}/\upmathrm{c m}^{2}$ in two linear determination ranges of 0.01–1 and $1{-}220~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and recoveries of $98\%-103\%$ [61]. MOFs composited are better sensors than incomposite MOFs due to their multifunctional sites and active surface areas. Fabricated MOFs can provide a stable platform with enhanced magnetic and electronic properties. For example, MOFs fabrication with mimicking-enzyme causes enhancement in magnetic properties that help recycle signal amplification. The designed material magnetic loop-DNA-NMOF-Pt (m-L-DNA) probes acted as a sensor for kanamycin (KANA) in milk, and catalytic hairpin assembly was assisted for signal amplification [62]. The deposition of gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) on MOFs helps to enhance the electromagnetic properties due to available electromagnetic sites [63]. Also, vitamins and other biomolecules with metal ions in MOFs frameworks provided efficient capturing sites for analytes in milk samples [64].
## 3. Electrochemical monitoring of milk samples
MOFs electrochemical biosensors have been developed as advanced integrated analytical devices laced with an electrochemical transducer for analytical responses  [58]. Also, due to the redox process, the electrochemical signals can be determined by catalytic reduction and affinities between analytes and MOFs [65]. The advanced features of electrochemical biosensors allowed them to design portable and fast signal-responsive materials for food quality monitoring with cost-effectiveness and high sensitivity. These advanced features made electrochemical biosensors promising food quality monitoring materials and alternatives to conventional techniques (Figure 3) [66].
Electrochemical transduction strategies such as potentiometric, voltammetric, and impedimetric are commonly used for biomarker detection. In addition, the electrode surface modification strategy involves electron transfer between an electrode, solution, and the detected molecules [67]. For example, the increasing use of UiO-66-OH ( $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs) with multifunctional groups for microextraction of trace polybrominated diphenyl ethers from milk created interest in multifunctional MOFs design [68]. Also, MOFs have been developed to detect peptides in goat milk samples. For example, MOF@MG, due to its porosity and magnetic properties, has utilized a better sensor for peptide detection in milk samples than ultrafiltration [69].
Streptomycin (STR) is commonly used in animal feed, and its concentration in milk can cause severe ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity [70]. Recently Peng et al. designed $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs composited with Ti3C2 aptasensor using the impregnation method to detect STR in milk samples rapidly. The nanocomposites in MOF enhanced electrochemical activity for detection STR with LOD 0.0033 nM in the range of 0.01 to $200\mathrm{nM}$ . Interestingly, milk sample analysis performance with reproducibility, stability, and selectivity for the STR was determined with recoveries ranging from $94.0\%$ to $105.0\%$ (Figure 4) [71].
Salmonella bacteria can occur in raw milk and disseminates in the product through contamination even after heating [72]. Recently, Feng et al. worked on designing a bimetallic CoFe-MOFs-graphene nanocomposite with a large specific surface area and high porosity. The graphene and multielectron sites in the composite are responsible for excellent electrical conductivity with enhanced current signals. Moreover, AuNPs and Anti-Salmonella antibodies were fabricated on the material surface for electrode construction by forming an $\mathrm{\Au{-NH}}_{2}$ bond. The designed materials detected Salmonella in milk with a LOD of $1.2\times10^{2}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ ranging from $2.4\times10^{2}$ CFU/mL to $2.4\times10^{8}$ CFU/mL [73]. Another bimetallic Co/Ni-MOF-based NiCo2O4 $@$ C/GCE composite actively detects furazolidone (FZD) and chloramphenicol (CAP) with LOD of $8.47\mathrm{nM}$ for FZD and $35{\mathrm{nM}}$ in ranges $0.5\mathrm{-}240\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for FZD and $0.5\mathrm{-}320\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for CAP. The hollow and mesoporous NiCo2O4@C MOF is abundant with carbon matrixes, oxygen defects, and valence metallic sites that have enhanced electroactivity and analytes detection accompanied by four-electron and four-proton processes. In addition, the designated sensor obtained recoveries for FZD $(95.85\%-103.9\%)$ and CAP $(95.72\%-104.4\%)$ in milk and honey [74].
It should be an important aim to enhance the electrochemical properties and activities of MOFs milk monitoring materials. In this context, the electrochemical properties of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs can be altered with the adsorption of $\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{6}{}^{3+}$ (RuHex) on the electrode surface. MOF backfilled with $\mathrm{Ru(NH}_{3})_{6}{}^{3+}$ and fabricated with thiolated cDNA/aptamer duplexes (dsDNA), 6-mercapto-1-hexanol and AuNPs exhibited sensitive detection of streptomycin (STR) with LOD of $2.6\mathrm{pg/mL}$ in the linear range of $0.005{-}150\mathrm{ng/mL}$ (Figure 5) [75]. Further, label-free DNA detection from milk samples has been used as a promising strategy for milk quality monitoring. In addition, active electron functionalities detected bacterial species like Salmonella, typhimurium (S. typhimurium). The electrode can be developed with methyl- $\upbeta$ - cyclodextrin $(\mathrm{M}\upbeta{\mathrm{CD}})$ , and AuNPs composited bimetallic Fe/Mn MOF spread on carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs). That acted as an electrochemical impedance electrochemical genosensor for detecting ss-tDNA with LOD of $0.07\ \mathrm{pM}$ in the linear range of $1\ \mathsf{p M}^{-1}\ \upmu\mathrm{M}$ resulting in ss-tDNA (single-stranded target DNA), with recoveries of $95.6\mathrm{-}104\%$ in milk samples [76].
