In MOFs electrochemical detection study, it can be observed that functionalities in the frameworks affect the food analytes depending on their electronic and charge properties [77]. For example, the reduction reaction is followed by metal ions in magnetic beads antibody fabricated Mn-MOF-74 designed by Wang to quickly detect Listeria monocytogenes (L. m) in milk. The developed detection model follows the process from $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ released by reduction reaction mediated by hydrogen peroxide in MOF that generates high electrochemical signals. Consequently, modified antibodies (Ab1) with magnetic beads $(\mathbf{MBs}@\mathbf{Abl}$ ) worked to separate L. m cells. Then Mn-MOF- $74@$ Ab2 was added, forming a sandwich complex (MBs@Ab1-L. m-Mn-MOF- $74@$ Ab2), leading to ultrasensitive electrochemical impedance detection of L. m in the concentration between $1.0\times100$ and $1.0\times104\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ is $90.2\%-$ $101.7\%$ in water and $88.5\%-96.2\%$ in milk. The detection limit of this approach for L. m cells in water and milk is 7.1 and $9.2\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ , respectively [78].
Moreover,  the development of new approaches to improve signals for electrochemical MOF sensors should focus on rapid food analysis [79]. The electrochemical triple amplification signal strategy is highly beneficial for detecting drugs and other analytes using MOFs [80]. For example, Zhang et al. developed the active surface of $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZnNi-MOF}$ with attached DNA probes with Au & Pt coated hollow cerium and polyethyleneimine molybdenum disulfide (PEIMoS2). Then aptamer is released from the double-stranded structure and DNAzyme cleavages RNA with the help of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in the presence of enrofloxacin (ENR). Moreover, Au@ZnNi-MOF catalyzed thionine (Thi) amplifies the signals and electrochemical detection of ENR in the range of $5.0\times10^{-6}$ to $1.0\times10^{-2}\mathrm{ng/mL}$ with a limit of detection of $1.02\times10^{-7}\mathrm{ng/mL}$ . Further, it has been successfully used in real environmental water and milk samples and has shown satisfactory performances [81].
Some electrochemical sensors can suffer from binder's adverse effects that reduce the detection efficiency. This limitation can be resolved using binder-free MOF materials for rapid food analysis [82]. For example, cobalt-doped Ni-MOF (Co/Ni-MOF) electrodeposited on a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) is used for detecting levofloxacin (LEV) without any conductive agent. However, cyclic voltammetric studies resulted in trace detection of LEV with LOD of $22{\mathrm{nM}}$ and a sensitivity of $2.85\upmu\mathrm{A}/\upmu\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in a wide linear range of $0.1\ \upmu\mathrm{M}-500\ \upmu\mathrm{M}$ with recoveries of $94.0\%-108.2\%$ in milk samples. In addition, the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions'
234 synergistic effect enhanced electron transfer and electrochemical activity of Co/Ni-MOF   
235 (Figure 6) [83].
MOFs, polypyrrole $({\mathrm{Ppy}})$ , and molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) composite with AuNPs act as a highly efficient biosensing materials [84]. For example, CoFe-MOFs with AuNPs worked as a sensor for tracing. Cyclic voltammograms detection of norfloxacin (NOR) on designed MIP/CoFe-MOFs/AuNPs/GCE. The fabrication of MOF provided an effective and enlarged surface area of $0.1058~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Moreover, CoFe-MOFs/AuNPs with more imprinted cavities exhibits LOD of 0.131 pmol/L with a wider linear range (5–100, 100–1000, and 1000–
6000 pmol/L) recovery rates between 97.04 and $110.2\%$ . On the other hand, the cyclic voltammetry and electrochemical impedance voltammetry electrochemical detection showed an irreversible electrochemical oxidation reaction from the controlled adsorption-diffusion response of an electron and a proton [85]. Nowadays, electrochemical aptasensing strategies for detecting Staphylococcus aureus, Escherichia coli O157:H7, and Salmonella enteric bacteria from food samples have gained interest due to fast signal changes and aptasensing. Aptamers immobilized poly Mn-MOFs constructed with a 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid, $^{4,4^{\prime}-}$ bipyridine, and polyvinyl pyrrolidone as a structural regulator, detect foodborne pathogens (SE, S. aureus, and E. coli) with LODs of 2.6, 1.5, and $3.5\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ in a wide linear range of 10 - 1 $\times10^{-8}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ [86].
Moreover, the insertion of PtNPs in MIL–101(Fe) for aflatoxin M1 (AFM1) electrochemical detection on a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) exhibited ultra-trace detection with LOD of $2.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{ng/mL}$ in a linear calibration range of $1.0\times10^{-2}$ to $\mathrm{80.0\ng/mL}$ . The metal NPs acted as electrochemical activity enhancers on the surface for electrochemical detection [87]. A Ni-MOF and AuNPs combined material exhibited sensitive detection for enrofloxacin with LOD of $5.6\times10^{-3}\mathrm{pg/mL}$ in a linear range of $1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{{pg/mL}}$ to $1\times10^{3}$ $\mathrm{{\pg/mL}}$ . The analytical performances of the sensor $\operatorname{\lambdauNP}@\operatorname{Ni-M}$ OF could be increased by loading aptamer (Apt) and synergistically catalyzing thionine (Thi). The fabrication also enhanced the sensitivity, repeatability, and stability required to develop effective food sensors for monitoring food quality [88]. In exploring MOFs electrochemical sensing capacity, the synergy of noble metal Au-doped coral-like $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ composited Co/Ni-Bio-MOF was developed. It detects kanamycin with a LOD of $0.037~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ in the range of $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $1000~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and reproducibility of $0.079\mathrm{-}0.63\%$ . The composite material showed reduced Differential Pulse Voltammetry response. It can be due to electron transfer hindrance on electrode surface for kanamycin detection. Also, the applied force disconnected $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}@$ Au-linked aptamer from the complementary sequence of composite MOF due to the applied force. It enhanced electron transfer and enabling signal amplification that is responsible for high working efficiency of designed model [89]. Hence MOFs materials have been developed and designed for electrochemical detection of contaminants from milk samples. The detection potential, sensitivity and recoveries could be the function of deposited materials and electron transfer process (Table 1).
## 4. Luminescence monitoring of milk samples
Luminescence MOF sensors have been developed with high sensitivity for detecting analytes from food samples [90]. The high luminescence properties of MOFs due to the presence of metal centers and active electronic functional sites support emission at various wavelengths [91]. Therefore, understanding the available sites light absorption and emission properties that altered upon interacting with guest analytes is critical. Moreover, transition metals, lanthanides, and acid-pyridine ligands constituted MOFs that exhibited particular optical properties explored for food samples analysis [92,93].
For example, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Oxybis(benzoic acid) and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Bipyridine (Bpy) linked Co(II)-MOFs with nanoflakes due to $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})6\mathrm{Mo}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{24}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ emitted light at $440\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $297\mathrm{nm}$ and was utilized for vanillin detection. Interestingly, the Co-MOFs' morphology did not affect photoluminescence emission intensity [94]. The design of materials by employing $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) polymers as modifiers with (NH2-MIL101(Fe)) exhibited the enhanced capturing of multianalyte from milk samples. The enhanced efficiency gained by SiO2:PVP@NH2-MIL(Fe) coating on NiFe2O4 provided an active surface [95]. MOFs sensing properties and mechanisms are the function of their functional groups and electronic sites. For example, MOFs ratiometric fluorescent for riboflavin (RF) detection follows the FRET mechanism. The presence of graphitic carbon nitrides quantum dots ( $\mathrm{\dot{\boldsymbol{g}}}$ -CNQDs) composited
Zn-MOF (g-CNQDs@Zn-MOF) exhibited high luminescence intensity and altered RF with ratiometric signal changes. The MOFs acted as a donor while RF  acted as an acceptor in the FRET system for high sensitivity and selectivity with a LOD of $15\mathrm{{Nm}}$ (Figure 7) [96].
Moreover, optical detection in colorimetric changes has advanced features and is timesaving. It can help to develop rapid detection and easy handling methods [97]. For example, a metalloporphyrin and AuNPs combined with ZIF-8 (Zn-MOF) led to the cascade reaction between choline oxidase and composite material. It can be noticed by quick colorimetric changes for choline detection from milk powder samples [98]. The functional groups in MOFs frameworks could determine the nature of electronic transitions. Moreover, the functional sites are key for detecting food sample analytes. For example, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) structure with groups of −NH2 and −COOH exhibited high-sensitivity fluorescence detection for tetracyclines (TCs) in milk. The active functionalities $-\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{-COOH}$ in NH2-MIL-53(Al) showed interactions with tetracyclines (TCs) from $-\mathbf{CO-}/\mathrm{-OH}$ by electron transfer from ligand to the $-\mathrm{CO-}/\mathrm{-OH}$ sites of TCs by hydrogen bonding. The inner filter effect (IFE) and photoinduced electron transfer (PET) account for fluorescence quenching and signal changes [99].
It should be mentioned that MOF nanoenzyme can be utilized for chromogenic reactions for H2O2 quantification [100]. For example, m-phthalic acid (PA) decorated PA-Tb-Cu MOF nanoenzyme used for fluorescence catalytic reaction of hydrogen peroxide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ . The $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and bridging ligand into a single material nanoenzyme detected $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with LOD of $0.2\upmu\mathrm{M}$ during catalysis with recoveries $91.50{-}108.5\%$ in milk samples. The detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ presented a strategy from ions/molecules fluorescence and catalytic activity with artificial enzymes [101]. In another example, Liu et al. designed a Tb-MOF for sensing and fluorescent quenching detection of tetracycline (TC) in milk. The designed material emitted a robust green fluorescence with a lifetime of $906.48\pm1.49~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ and a 2 min fast response for the TC detection. It exhibited a LOD of $184\mathrm{nmol}/\mathrm{L}$ in the range of 0–100 and $100{-}244~\mathrm{\upmumol/L}$ with recoveries of $93.5\mathrm{-}104.2\%$ for TC. Luminescence quenching was from the inner-filter effect and energy transfer from ligand to $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion [102].
Trichothecene mycotoxins cause immunotoxicity and are commonly found in food samples. Hence rapid detection and establishment of high-sensitivity and reliable methods can help monitor the quality  [103]. For example, Zhao et al. designed NH2–UiO–66 for fluorescence quenching T–2 toxin detection with LOD of $0.239\mathrm{ng/ml}$ in the range of 0.5–100 $\mathrm{ng/ml}$ , and recoveries of milk and beer were $89.86\mathrm{-}108.99\%$ . The high quenching efficiency of the sensor via Cy3–labeled aptamer (Cy3–aptamer) and the observed $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction, hydrogen bond, and coordination based on FRET and PET (Figure 8) [104]. In another example, Jalili et al. designed a ratiometric fluorescent probe based on in situ incorporation. The insertion of AuNCs and CDs into ZIF-8 is responsible for enhanced luminescence signals that alter ratiometrically on sensing for Cephalexin (CFX). For example, the observed dualemissions centered at 520 and $630\mathrm{nm}$ when excited at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ were quenched by CFX when applied to detect CFX in the milk samples [105].
Long-term ingestion of melamine causes renal malfunction and metabolic problems in the urinary system [106]. Zr-MOFs immobilized with tris(2,2-bipyridyl) ruthenium (II) chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{\langle[Ru(bpy)3]^{2+}})$ ( $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH_{2}}@\mathrm{Ru}$ acted as a ratiometric fluorescence sensor for melamine with dual emission signals at 445 and $595\mathrm{nm}$ (excitation of $350\mathrm{nm}$ ). The corresponding two peaks at $445\mathrm{nm}$ were enhanced and at $595\mathrm{nm}$ quenched in the presence of melamine as an "on-off" ratiometric fluorescence sensor. The designed MOF exhibited a LOD of $90{\mathrm{nM}}$ in the range of 0.27 to $110~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [107]. In another example of fluorescence detection, Zr-porphyrin MOF (PCN-222) interacted with chloramphenicol (CAP) through $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, electrostatic, hydrogen bond, and coordination interactions. The observed high quenching efficiency by FRET and PET processes demonstrates the electronic and energy transfers. The designed model showed a LOD of $0.08\mathrm{pg/mL}$ in the range of $0.1\mathrm{pg/mL}$ to $10\mathrm{ng/mL}$ for chloramphenicol (CAP) in milk and shrimp samples [108]. In another example, Khataee et al. designed AuNCs and copper nanoclusters composited MOF for ratiometric detection
Tetracycline (Tcy) antibiotic. AuCuNCs@MOF exhibited dual emission peaks at 520 and 650 nm (excitation wavelength $400~\mathrm{nm}$ ) that got quenched at $615~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and unchanged at $520~\mathrm{nm}$ with Tetracycline (Tcy). The material can detect Tetracycline (Tcy) with a LOD of $4.8\ \mathrm{nM}$ in the range of 20 to $650\mathrm{nM}$ [109].
Pyrraline high concentration of contaminating milk can cause diabetes and affect the health of people [110]. A $\mathrm{Cr}$ -MOFs coated with a molecularly imprinted polymer ((MIL-101 $@$ DMIP) enriched with pyrrolidine-3-carboxylic acid has been used as a promising candidate for pyrraline due to MOFs' faster mass transport suitable for excellent sensitivity and selectivity [111]. Materials such as mixed-matrix-membrane exhibited a large surface area of 1322.09 $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and pore volume of $0.65\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . Hence fabrication with MIL-101(Cr) MOFs can provide an efficient surface for bisphenol A (BPA) from milk samples with ultra-low detection of 16 ng/ in the range of $0.1\sim50~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ with recoveries of $74.2\%$ to $110.6\%$ [112]. Furthermore, the residues of contaminants in milk contaminants may transfer to the human body, which can adversely affect the human endocrine system. Therefore, the rapid and acute analysis of these contaminants with imperative MOF sensors will develop rapid and effective approaches to contaminate extraction from milk at trace levels.
Formaldehyde is widely used in milk factories and can become the main contaminant in milk [113]. MOFs toxicant monitoring activity has been improved by inserting $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions to enhance luminescence properties. Li et al. designed a 3D ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}@{\mathrm{Ag-MOFs}}$ for fluorescence sensing of formaldehyde in solvents and paper. The designed MOF acted as an efficient turnon sensor induced by $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , showing a dynamic range of $0.1\mathrm{-}1~\mathrm{mM}$ with a LOD of $1.9~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Furthermore, the designed MOF with polyacrylonitrile acted as a formaldehyde remover from milk with a removal rate of $98.52\%$ . In addition, the designed MOF worked as fluorescence test papers when observed under $365\mathrm{nm}$ UV lamp light and showed a selective testing response (Figure 9) [114].
The design of a new MOF sensor for the selective determination of contaminants in milk is challenging [115]. Indicating the selection of particular ligands, for instance, squaramide in Co-MOF is helpful for the selective detection of lactose among various monosaccharides and disaccharides. The luminescence intensity of Co-MOF got enhaned with lactose due to the interaction between lactose and squaramide parts of MOF. However, the interaction caused inhibition of PET due to decreased electron transfer from the nitrogen lone pair to the $\pi$ orbitals [116]. In addition, compounds like pyrophosphate showed a coordination effect with the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-88B(Fe) MOF caused, causing a structural collapse and releasing diaminoterephthalic acid. This process weakening of the ligand-metal charge transfer (LMCT) of the MOFs, and the original fluorescence of the ligand is recovered. Wang et al. designed MOFs $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL88B(Fe) sensor for target recognition of coupling anti-chloramphenicol (CAP) monoclonal antibodies in milk samples with the LOD of $0.028~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ in the range of $0.05{-}0.75~\mathrm{\upmug/L}$ with a recovery rate of $91.8\%-112.3\%$ [117].
Despite the previous discussion, it is critical to developing new heterostructure composites like MOF-on-MOF materials [118]. These materials have high porosity and enlarged surface area due to the composition of MOFs. For example, Wang et al. designed MOF $@$ MOF, ZIF$8@$ PCN-128Y (Zn-MOF $@$ Zr-MOF) composite materials by an internal extension growth that acted as a turn-off sensor for method tetracycline with LOD of $60{\mathrm{nM}}$ in the range of 0.4 to 80 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and recovery rate of $94.2\mathrm{-}106.3\%$ in milk samples. In addition, the composite material is enriched with $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ metal centers and ligand functional sites that provide an optical tentacle with advanced luminescence properties (Figure 10) [119]. Moreover, undeniable and advanced progress has been made in developing composite with carbon dots. For example, the composition of carbon dots on Ag-MOF exhibited excellent stability and optical property with high fluorescence intensity. The fluorescence intensity got quenched with chloramphenicol at $427~\mathrm{nm}$ (at the excitation wavelength of $332~\mathrm{nm}$ ) due to the PET. As a result, the MOF composited showed an ultra-trace detection limit of $44~\mathrm{nM}$ with satisfied recoveries (104 to $109\%$ and good precision $(<4\%)$ [120].
Moreover, the luminescence intensity signal changes can demonstrate the relationship between the structure and detectability. Furthermore, luminescence spectra' shape and structure can provide insight into guest adsorption, Skelton damage, host-guest interaction, and light energy competition that occurs upon MOFs interaction with analytes [121]. Hence, MOF sensors fluorescence signals could be explained based on emission intensity on, off, ratiometric, and “on-off-on” responses. Moreover, the quantification of analytes and MOFs detection potential on a wide range can be elaborated in commercial and industrial applications (Table 2) [122].
## 5. Opportunities in Food Sensors
MOFs fabricated with nanomaterials have enhanced properties such as binding strength, rapid binding kinetics, high selectivity, and sensitivity, working as different pHs of sample solution, real samples analysis, etc. [123]. Moreover, acrylic resin contained 3D-printed electrodes that acted for electrochemical sensor sulphanilamide (SAA) determination in milk samples. The analytical tool of the 3D-printed electrodes may not need any laborious samples for routine analysis.[124]. MOF synthetic methods could determine the analytes capturing properties from milk samples. It is necessary to control the magnetic, charge, and structural properties of MOFs for analytes of analysis  [125]. Also, immobilization with multivalent metal ions and polymerization by employing acidic and amide-based functionalities (vinylphosphonic acid and N,N-methylenebisacrylamide) can be helpful to improve the sensitivity for analytes detection [126].
Moreover, electrochemical MOFs with oxidase-integrated, peroxidase-like activity and one-step cascade detection of analytes can avoid intermediate formation and transformation associated with multi-step detection [127]. In addition, natural enzyme immobilization with inherent MOFs enhanced the peroxidase mimic activity and stability for electrochemical detection of analytes like glutamate oxidase from milk samples [128]. MOFs designed with other porous materials, such as covalent organic frameworks and fiber coating, can exhibit improved stability and reusability for determining analytes from milk samples. This can be due to the high strength of COFs and fibers that maintain stable mechanical properties after several cycles [129]. Hence, MOFs structural properties determine the analytical performance. The modification in MOFs architecture with porous, stable, and active surface materials will give a new class of durable and reusable porous materials for the acute determination of analytes.
## 6. Challenges in Food Sensors
MOFs in milk quality monitoring to ensure quality and safety have provided a platform to detect toxicant contaminates [130–132]. MOFs as coordinated materials and composites with nanomaterials and biomolecules are considered to improve sensing properties for detecting analytes [133]. However, it still suffers some challenges, and it is necessary to put more effort into covering the challenges that remain for real-life applications. Nevertheless, it is wellknown material with real-life applications that can provide new devices for milk safety monitoring, even in remote areas, by covering challenges [134]. MOF sensors can discriminate the milk samples' contamination presence but cannot conclude that milk is safe.
1.  The discrimination between molecules with similar chemistry, such as methanol and ethanol, is still challenging. For example, it is well known that methanol is poisonous and can be an undesired product in milk through any fermentation process.   
2. Milk packaging and shelf life can be challenging for quality monitoring.   
3. Establishing $\mathsf{p H}$ for milk with time and temperature is another quality monitoring and validation challenge.   
4. MOFs reusability and biocompatibility are still challenging for milk safety and quality monitoring. It also limits the MOFs applications at large scale for milk quality analysis. In addition, the toxicity of metal ions, ligand molecules, and fabricated materials can affect the samples. Hence, using non-toxic and nature-based products to design new MOFs could overcome this major challenge.
Milk quality measurement at the commercial level can create a barrier to regulation and standardization. Milk quality sampling and monitoring in remote areas can be overcome by developing portable MOF sensors. Although the researchers have explored MOF sensors, developing intelligent sensors and digital monitoring analysis by designing MOF sensors is still challenging. Hence, further research will be required to ensure food safety regulatory and enforcement agencies for maturity scale food safety. MOFs evaluation should be carried out commercially to develop a user-centered monitoring kit.
## 7. Conclusion and outcomes
This study provided MOF design and applications for detecting analytes from milk samples. Electrochemical and spectroscopic techniques are efficient tools for monitoring milk quality. In addition, MOFs with biomolecules, carbon materials, and MOFs can provide an active platform with enhanced electronic and physical-chemical properties. Furthermore, MOFs can be designed for particular analyte monitoring in milk by modifying the synthesis methods and ligands. Therefore, the study illustrated MOFs and hybrid materials that were efficient for monitoring toxic chemicals such as metal ions, anions, drugs, dyes, volatile organic compounds, pesticides, herbicides, etc., from milk samples.
MOFs are promising candidates for monitoring milk quality. It is shown that electrochemical and luminescence MOF materials have served the purpose of sensitive detection of contaminants explicitly. Then, post-modification in MOFs structure with loading nanoparticles and biomolecules can be used to design the final sensing platform. The designed platform shows promising applications when applied to real milk samples analysis. Finally, MOF-based high-sensitive, stable, reusable, and non-toxic materials will be required, especially for milk quality monitoring to ensure food quality.
It is critical that MOF materials as milk quality monitoring devices can be used for real samples at the industrial level. Likewise, the potential of MOFs materials for milk quality and safety should be focused on and explored in actual analysis and milk applications. Hence the next generation of proof-of-concept studies needs to be designed and developed for milk applications and target analysis of contaminants.
